I have watched ahead to AARM Part 1 and 2.
我已经提前看了《AARM》的第一和第二部分。

169
00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:55.560
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

170
00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:56.900
You and Rain.
你和雷恩。

171
00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:07.060
Your combination of, like, emotion and comedy in these episodes leading to the finale is so amazing.
你们在这些通往大结局的剧集中，情感与喜剧的结合真是太棒了。

172
00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:11.440
I mean, I'm going to get to yours later in this breakdown, but so good, Angela.
我是说，我稍后会在解析中提到你的部分，但安吉拉，真的太棒了。

173
00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:12.380
Oh, my God.
天哪。

174
00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:13.240
Thanks, lady.
谢谢你，女士。

175
00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:18.260
Well, David says, I would like you to be manager.
大卫说，我想让你担任经理。

176
00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:20.100
Dwight already has a business card ready.
德怀特已经准备好了一张名片。

177
00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:20.760
I know.
我知道。

178
00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:21.840
You can't believe it.
你简直不敢相信。

179
00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:25.600
He had one ready just in case Michael or Andy ever got killed in a traffic accident,
他准备好一张名片，以防迈克尔或安迪在交通事故中丧生，

180
00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.780
and there was a trade show on the exact same day.
而且那天正好有贸易展览会。

181
00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:29.500
He's a planner.
他是个有计划的人。

182
00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:31.040
He sure is.
他确实是。

183
00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:33.760
Listen, we got a fan question from Sammy B.
听着，我们收到了来自新罕布什尔州巴灵顿的萨米·B发来的粉丝提问，

184
00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.680
in Barrington, New Hampshire, who said,
他说，

185
00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:38.820
at what point was it decided that Dwight would be manager?
是在什么时候决定让德怀特担任经理的？

186
00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.780
It's the perfect culmination of his storyline.
这是他故事线的完美高潮。

187
00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:43.100
It really is.
确实如此。

188
00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:47.800
Well, basically, Sammy, as soon as they knew that the spinoff wasn't happening,
基本上，萨米，一旦他们知道衍生剧不会拍了，

189
00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:52.400
they knew that Dwight needed to end this series as manager of Dunder Mifflin.
他们就知道德怀特需要以邓德·米夫林经理的身份结束这个系列。

190
00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:58.140
I actually reached out to Greg, and he said that the discussions over how to end the show
我实际上联系了格雷格，他说关于如何结束这部剧的讨论，

191
00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.400
focused a lot on what each character deserved.
很大程度上集中在每个角色应得的结局上。

192
00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:07.520
Like, how could we make everyone's maybe make their wildest dreams come true,
比如，我们怎样才能让每个人的梦想成真，

193
00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.720
but also what do they deserve to get from this journey?
但同时他们从这段旅程中应得什么？

194
00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:15.060
And Greg said that Dwight was a man with very clear passions.
格雷格说，德怀特是一个有着非常明确热情的人。

195
00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.900
It was not hard to figure out what would make him happy.
不难看出什么能让他快乐。

196
00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.980
And he said most character arcs were decided about a quarter of the way through season nine
他说大多数角色的弧线是在第九季四分之一的时候决定的，

197
00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:25.540
so that they could slowly write toward all of them.
这样他们就可以慢慢地为所有角色铺垫结局。

198
00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:29.480
Well, I have to tell you guys, while I was in Canada filming this movie,
我得告诉你们，当我在加拿大拍这部电影时，

199
00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:34.940
Greg and I were texting, and we realized we were both in Vancouver on the same day.
格雷格和我发短信，我们意识到我们同一天都在温哥华。

200
00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.820
And we both realized this on our last day there.
而且我们是在那里的最后一天才意识到这一点的。

201
00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.460
So we were able to meet and have lunch.
所以我们得以见面并一起吃了午饭。

202
00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:41.420
It was so great.
真是太棒了。

203
00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:42.720
We hung out for two hours.
我们待了两个小时。

204
00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:43.520
We laughed.
我们笑得很开心。

205
00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:44.640
We talked about the show.
我们聊了这部剧。

206
00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:50.040
He is so happy for Office Ladies and for what we've done in the rewatch, Jenna.
他为《办公室女士》以及我们在重温节目中所做的一切感到非常高兴，珍娜。

207
00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:55.040
And I hope we get to have him on again because he's just rooting for us.
我希望我们能再次邀请他来，因为他真的很支持我们。

208
00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.120
And he's like, Ange, where are you in season nine?
他还问我，安吉，你们看到第九季哪里了？

209
00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:58.220
Tell me everything.
快告诉我所有细节。

210
00:08:58.960 --> 00:08:59.780
I love him.
我太喜欢他了。

211
00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:00.520
I know.
我知道。

212
00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:04.720
You know, he told me that when they start filming this spinoff,
你知道吗，他告诉我，当他们开始拍摄这部衍生剧时，

213
00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:11.300
that we can come to the set and get a behind-the-scenes, I guess, view of all the goings-on.
我们可以去片场，我想，去看看幕后的所有运作情况。

214
00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:12.700
And I can't wait.
我真是迫不及待了。

215
00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:14.460
And we get to tell you guys about it.
到时候我们就能告诉你们大家了。

216
00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:14.880
Yeah.
没错。

217
00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:18.300
Well, speaking of behind-the-scenes, how do you like that segue?
说到幕后，你觉得这个转场怎么样？

218
00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:22.060
Angela's doing something behind-the-scenes under her desk.
安吉拉正在桌子底下搞什么幕后动作呢。

219
00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:24.820
She is pouring—is it vodka?
她在倒——是伏特加吗？

220
00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:31.240
It's a clear alcohol into her big 7-Eleven super gulp is what it looks like.
看起来像是把某种透明酒精倒进了她那个巨大的7-Eleven超大杯里。

221
00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:32.820
She's basically become Meredith.
她简直变成了梅雷迪思。

222
00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:33.540
Yes!
没错！

223
00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:34.260
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

224
00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:37.540
She's got a lot going on, and she's not handling it very well.
她最近事情很多，而且处理得不太好。

225
00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:41.360
Meanwhile, Pam goes back to the Annex to visit Jim.
与此同时，帕姆回到办公区去找吉姆。

226
00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:44.400
She has a question for him, but she can't remember what it is.
她有个问题想问他，但却想不起来是什么了。

227
00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:47.980
Oh, this is such, like, back-to-Jim-and-Pam flirty days.
噢，这简直就像回到了吉姆和帕姆打情骂俏的日子。

228
00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:50.940
I love that they rack focus to Toby.
我喜欢镜头焦点转到托比那一段。

229
00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:52.480
He's in agony.
他痛苦极了。

230
00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:54.220
Yes.
没错。

231
00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:59.160
It was so fun to finally get to play Happy Jim-Pam again.
能再次扮演快乐的吉姆和帕姆真是太有趣了。

232
00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:00.400
Yeah, I bet.
是啊，我敢肯定。

233
00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:05.760
And now when I'm re-watching it and I'm seeing it, I see how very long it has been.
现在重温这部剧时，我才发现这已经是好久以前的事了。

234
00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:08.460
Andy is now going to address the whole bullpen.
安迪现在要向整个办公区发表讲话了。

235
00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:13.280
He announces he's decided to stay at Dunder Mifflin and transition to sales.
他宣布决定留在邓德·米夫林公司，并转岗到销售部。

236
00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:16.060
Nellie thinks this could be his best role yet.
奈莉认为这可能是他迄今为止最适合的角色。

237
00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:18.440
Everyone's very supportive of this idea.
大家都非常支持这个想法。

238
00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:19.020
Yeah.
没错。

239
00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:22.520
I need you all, though, to go to 4 minutes and 26 seconds.
不过，我需要你们大家去看一下4分26秒处。

240
00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:23.600
What will I see?
我会看到什么？

241
00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.140
Well, I have a Dundee to give out in this scene.
嗯，在这个场景里我要颁发一个邓迪奖。

242
00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:26.920
What is it?
是什么奖？

243
00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:34.300
It's the hair-ific Dundee for most beautiful hair in the office, and it goes to Nellie.
是“发型惊艳奖”，颁给办公室里头发最美的人，获奖者是奈莉。

244
00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:35.340
Yes!
太棒了！

245
00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:37.880
I meant to mention this last week.
我上周就想提这件事了。

246
00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:46.300
There was that scene of her and Kevin in Andy's office, and her hair is so voluminous.
在安迪办公室里她和凯文的那场戏中，她的头发看起来太蓬松了。

247
00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.360
It is, like, what a blowout.
没错，那吹发造型做得真好。

248
00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:49.340
What happened?
怎么做到的？

249
00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:52.040
I said this is, like, Victoria's Secret's model hair.
我说这简直就是维多利亚的秘密模特的头发。

250
00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:54.140
You know when they stand in front of the fan?
你知道她们站在风扇前那种效果吗？

251
00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:54.580
Yes!
太棒了！

252
00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:55.020
Yes!
太棒了！

253
00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:02.060
I looked in the script to see if there was a deleted storyline that pointed to why her hair is so terrific.
我查了剧本，想看看有没有什么被删掉的情节解释为什么她的头发那么赞。

254
00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:03.860
It is phenomenal.
简直绝了。

255
00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:05.320
Hair day for Nellie.
内莉的秀发日。

256
00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:08.120
Anyway, she gets a Dundee for hair-ific.
总之，她因为“发”现精彩获得了一个邓迪奖。

257
00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:08.780
Hello!
嘿！

258
00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:10.120
Did you see what I did there?
看到我刚才玩的梗了吗？

259
00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:10.840
I love it.
我喜欢。

260
00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:13.040
I love a hair pun.
我喜欢这种关于头发的谐音梗。

261
00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:15.260
Well, should we go back to the annex?
好了，我们要回附楼吗？

262
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.240
There's some cutie pie business happening right there.
那边正上演着甜甜蜜蜜的一幕呢。

263
00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:20.620
Pete is showing Erin a new way to use the stapler.
皮特正在教艾琳一种订书机的新用法。

264
00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:21.720
I didn't know this.
我以前都不知道。

265
00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:22.900
Is it a real thing?
这是真的吗？

266
00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:24.960
Yes, but when am I using this?
是啊，但我什么时候会用到这个呢？

267
00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:31.300
I've known this about the stapler all along, that you can switch the foot plate, and then
我一直都知道订书机可以这样用，你可以把底座转过来，然后

268
00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.520
instead of crimping in, it crimps out.
它订出来的钉脚不是向内扣，而是向外翻。

269
00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.960
When do I need to crimp out my staple?
我什么时候需要把钉脚向外翻呢？

270
00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:36.980
Is why?
为什么呢？

271
00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:39.720
Would you use it for, like, a poster on the wall or something?
你会用它来贴墙上的海报之类的吗？

272
00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:40.180
No.
不会。

273
00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:40.940
No.
不会。

274
00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:40.990
No.
不会。

275
00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:41.500
Hmm.
嗯。

276
00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.440
I would use...
我会用……

277
00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:43.760
Hmm.
嗯。

278
00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:45.380
I hadn't thought about it.
我还没想过这个问题。

279
00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:45.920
Yeah.
是啊。

280
00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:46.340
Hmm.
嗯。

281
00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:47.900
There's no use for it.
这东西没用。

282
00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:52.380
Someone please write in and tell me the use for the reverse crimp.
哪位能写信告诉我反向装订有什么用吗？

283
00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:53.340
I would like to know.
我很想知道。

284
00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:54.420
I would too.
我也是。

285
00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:56.240
I should have done a deep dive on it.
我本该深入研究一下的。

286
00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:57.200
Should I do it right now?
我现在查一下？

287
00:11:57.960 --> 00:11:58.500
Hold on.
等等。

288
00:11:58.700 --> 00:11:59.640
I'm going to Google it.
我来谷歌一下。

289
00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:00.640
All right.
好吧。

290
00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:01.580
I'm going to have some iced tea.
我去喝点冰茶。

291
00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:03.900
Why would I...
我为什么要……

292
00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:05.060
How are you going to word it?
你打算怎么措辞？

293
00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:08.660
Reverse crimp a staple.
反向装订订书钉。

294
00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:12.520
It looks like it's for temporary or permanent stapling.
看起来它是用于临时或永久装订的。

295
00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:14.720
One is easier to remove.
其中一种更容易拆除。

296
00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:16.860
Which one's easier to remove?
哪种更容易拆除？

297
00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:18.740
I guess the crimped outward one.
我猜是向外弯的那种。

298
00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:20.960
Is easier to remove than crimped in.
比向内弯的更容易拆除。

299
00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:24.580
The crimped area of a stapler is used to bend the ends of a staple into a secure shape
订书机的装订区用于将订书钉的两端弯曲成稳固的形状，

300
00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:25.740
that holds a paper together.
从而将纸张固定在一起。

301
00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:28.140
Okay, so...
好吧，那么……

302
00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:31.360
The plate that makes up for the crimp area can be adjusted for either temporary or
用于压痕区域的底板可以调节，以实现临时或

303
00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:32.340
permanent stapling.
永久装订。

304
00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:35.260
I mean, is any staple truly permanent?
我是说，真的有永久的装订吗？

305
00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:40.360
No, because there's that special little prongy thing that you can use to unstaple things.
没有，因为有个专门的小尖头工具可以用来拆钉子。

306
00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:45.380
Stapling with the crimp area set for outward bend staples results in the weaker but easier
将压痕区域设置为向外弯曲，装订出来的钉子虽然较弱，但

307
00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:46.260
to remove staple.
更容易拆除。

308
00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:48.220
Hmm.
嗯。

309
00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:52.860
Well, I feel like we learned a lot and nothing all at the same time.
好吧，我觉得我们好像学到了很多，但又好像什么都没学到。

310
00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:54.100
That was not satisfying at all.
这真是一点也不让人满足。

311
00:12:54.180 --> 00:13:00.460
I feel like someone really overthought the stapler when they designed it.
我觉得设计订书机的那个人真的想太多了。

312
00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:01.880
I'm not going to judge that person.
我不会去评判那个人。

313
00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:05.560
They wanted to give you options, but they didn't know the prongy thing was going to be
他们想给你提供选择，但他们当时还不知道那个小尖头工具会被

314
00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:05.920
invented.
发明出来。

315
00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:07.660
There you go.
就是这样。

316
00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:09.000
That's what happened.
事情就是这样发生的。

317
00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:13.740
I have an invention that I hope someone is working on.
我有一个发明，希望有人正在研究。

318
00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:14.620
What is that?
是什么？

319
00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:18.950
Is anyone working to make...
有没有人在研究怎么……

320
00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:22.600
What?
什么？

321
00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:24.020
You're so tickled.
你笑得好开心。

322
00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:24.660
What is it?
到底是什么？

323
00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:27.340
I really fired up last night about it.
我昨晚真的为此感到很激动。

324
00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:28.580
It's so dumb.
这太蠢了。

325
00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:31.080
I really had this thought.
我真的有这个想法。

326
00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:33.080
I thought, is anyone working on this?
我在想，有没有人在研究这个？

327
00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:35.280
Why are you angry about this?
你为什么对这个感到生气？

328
00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:41.660
Is anyone working on making bags of cereal easier to open?
有没有人在研究如何让麦片袋更容易打开？

329
00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:43.040
Why?
为什么？

